 (:adr/body "**This is the current resume point.** ADR-2609102000 carried the previous one;
its own resume section has been updated in place and now points here.

## What landed, and where

Everything below is on the relevant `main`, verified by ancestry rather than
by having pushed it:

    root         24f2067   what built-rust-raw names (+ orgs-detectors repair)
    root         4fcfd70   com-aave's Q9 gate, 4 of 12 to 10 of 12
    root         4c8e0e4   west pin com-aave ce72cba -> e08071a
    com-aave     e08071a   nbb reads nbb.edn, so the coordinate goes there

No pull requests were left open. Every branch and worktree this session created
was removed.

## 1. built-rust-raw: answered by reproduction

ADR-2609102000 listed three candidates and called it an owner decision. It is
the first -- **a real artifact built elsewhere** -- and it was measured:

    rebuilt with rustc 1.96.0     2118 bytes  ==  declared 2118
    computed bafkreidytpismfkcitfathhfhibefvg6o4tgseipdd6tcvwstayenaitye
    declared bafkreidytpismfkcitfathhfhibefvg6o4tgseipdd6tcvwstayenaitye

The unmodified control produced a **different** CID, which is the only reason
the agreement is evidence and not an echo.

What it names: one 101-line `no_std` Rust crate in
`kotoba-lang/cleanroom-browser-runtime/wasm-rs`, built 1,000 times, whose only
per-actor input is a 3-line `actor_meta.rs`. **999 distinct CIDs are not 999
distinct programs.** The artifact exports 9 functions over an untyped
`Vec<Vec<u8>>`; the manifests declare 20 REST routes and 20 MCP tools. The
404s are a declared-unfinished IPFS pin step, stated in the generating ADR's
own Remaining line -- not loss, not corruption, not a Kotoba problem.

## 2. com-aave: 4 of 12 to 10 of 12

Six verifications landed. `whole-public-surface` is 33/34 with one declared,
unreachable omission (`as-float`; aave's schema has zero float fields), and is
recorded as a stated exception rather than scored green.
**`consumer-resolution` is the only item left.**

The headline measurement: **`amu` and the public `kotoba` CLI produce
byte-identical artifacts** -- 22,078 bytes, identical provenance record, the
publication record differing only in the output filename it was told to use.

## Verification actually run, with counts

    rust builds (control + aave)             2   both validated by wasm-tools
    amu compile                              4   run1, run2, mutant, equivalence
    kotoba -M compile                        1
    capability policies exercised            4   both, clock-only, entropy-only, none
    com-aave repository tests                6 tests / 98 assertions / 0 failures
    kotobase bytes-plane probes              2   aave 404, control 200, same run
    verify-adr-identity                      exit 0 (twice)
    java/javac/clojure/clj invocations       0 in every measurement reported as
                                               JVM-free -- and 4 when the trap
                                               deliberately fired

**Not green, and not mine:** `docs-edn-check --strict-keys` still exits 1. Two
causes. `STRANGLER-FIG-MONTH1-DEPLOYMENT-SUMMARY.edn` has an unmatched
delimiter -- pre-existing, untouched. And 806 `.edn` are in git but outside the
sparse cone of a docs worktree, where the gate correctly refuses to report a
pass on files it could not read. The third cause was
`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`, and that one **is** fixed.

## New defects found, none of them fixed

These came out of the trap and the controls, and each is a separate change:

1. **The public `kotoba` CLI starts a JVM.** `check --source-path`, `inspect`
   and `definition-cids` shell out to `clojure -M:run`. The contract says
   `:public-cli {:jvm-launcher-forbidden true}`. `amu --jvm-free` fails closed
   as specified; the `kotoba` driver has no such flag on these paths.
2. **`kotoba -M check` on a relative path** reports `input could not be read`
   -- the identical message a genuinely missing file produces. This produced a
   wrong reading mid-session that would have declared the whole contract
   blocked.
3. **The contract documents an invocation that does not run.**
   `:public-cli :source-check` reads `kotoba check --safe <entry>`; the CLI
   requires the `-M` boundary and has no `--safe`.
4. **`:kotoba/admission-denied` does not name which effect was denied.** With
   two capabilities that is a short search. It will not stay short.
5. **`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` had two unescaped quotes** that closed
   `:verify-itonami-run-path`'s `:why` early, leaving four symbol keys and a
   truncated string that read clean. Fixed here; the class is the one this
   workspace keeps rediscovering.

## Resume point, in priority order

1. **`consumer-resolution` for com-aave** -- the last of the twelve. Note what
   ADR-2609103000 established: there is no working incumbent artifact and no
   external consumer of `aave.main`, so nothing is being regressed. Removing
   the obstacle is not the same as doing the work.
2. **The four malformed `ns` forms** (kouhou, shinshi-growth-actor, tashikame,
   wt-kouhou). Deliberately left unfixed until a detector existed;
   `scripts/verify-ns-form-loadable.cljs` exists now. Untouched this session.
3. **The public CLI's JVM fallback (defect 1 above).** It sits directly under
   `kotoba only`: half the contract's build path is not JVM-free, and nothing
   was measuring it because nobody ran the public CLI under a trap.
4. **`jvm-build` 452 if the goal is repository count; `jvm-source` 207 if the
   goal is the language.** Unchanged from the previous resume point. They are
   different goals and the owner has said both. **`jvm-runtime-deps` is still
   the wrong axis** -- it removes third-party JVM libraries, not the JVM.

## Owner decisions still open, none of which an agent should take

- **Whether 1,000 manifests may keep advertising capabilities their artifacts
  do not implement.** This is the substantive finding of ADR-2609103000 and a
  truthfulness question about published manifests, not a build question.
- **Whether to pin that corpus as-is.** The bytes reproduce on demand, so
  pinning is cheap -- but it makes 1,000 stubs retrievable at addresses whose
  manifests overstate them. Deliberately not done here.
- **Rust provenance against ADR-2607072000**, which says Rust is not written in
  kotoba-lang. The crate producing all 1,000 artifacts lives there today.
- **Whether com-aave's `.nbb/` cache should be committed.** Untracked and
  unignored. Committing it makes the JVM-free test path work on a cold
  checkout; the directory is hash-named per resolved dependency set.
- Carried forward, unchanged: who consumes
  `did:web:etzhayyim.com:actor:aave-compat`; whether `weight-kototama`'s stored
  snapshot prefixes may be re-addressed; whether wave-1's 20 pilots are
  re-qualified or retired.

## Left alone deliberately, belonging to other sessions

Reported rather than cleaned, per the rule that a closing session tidies only
its own work:

    15 codex/* worktrees + branches      register-*, chicory-*, migrate-nbb-edn,
                                         kotoba-native-commands, spotwork-extract
    3 stashes                            rule-kaizen x2, kaizen ledger WIP
    3 non-codex worktrees                itonami-growth-bot, wt-hp, wt-tsukuru-scout
    orgs/kotoba-lang/amu                 2 modified docs files, 37 behind its pin
    orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang         commits on bot/migrate-ledger-tick68

The superproject checkout is on a **detached HEAD, 25 behind origin/main**. It
was already detached at session start and is the shared integration checkout,
where the standing rule forbids branch switching. Left as found.

## Reproduce

Both prior ADRs carry their own reproduce sections and they still hold. The one
thing worth repeating because it cost time: **the trap must be proven to fire
before an empty trace means anything**, and in this session it fired twice on
paths nobody predicted -- the public CLI, and nbb's own dependency resolution
shelling out to babashka. A trace never tripped is indistinguishable from a
trace that cannot trip.")))
